var tafsirData_8 = {
  "1": "'They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the spoils of war. Say, \"Their distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger. So be mindful of Allah, settle your affairs, and obey Allah and His Messenger if you are ˹true˺ believers.\"' (Quran 8:1). The surah opens on an argument.\n\n'The spoils of war' — al-anfal. The chapter is named after this word, and it means additions — property gained beyond what was expected.\n\nThe occasion is Badr, fought in the second year after the migration to Madinah. A small Muslim force met a Meccan army roughly three times its size and won, and afterwards there was a dispute over who owned what was left on the field.\n\n'They ask you regarding the spoils' — yas'alunaka. The question is put to the Prophet, peace be upon him, because the claimants could not settle it themselves.\n\n'Their distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger' — qul al-anfalu lillahi wal-rasul. The answer removes the property from the dispute entirely.\n\nNotice what that does. Nobody's claim is judged; the basis of claiming is withdrawn.\n\n'So be mindful of Allah, settle your affairs' — wa aslihu dhata baynikum. Repair what is between you.\n\n'Islah' — the word running through al-A'raf at 7:35, 7:56 and 7:142. The first command of this surah is to fix a relationship, not to divide an estate.\n\n'And obey Allah and His Messenger if you are true believers' — the condition is placed at the end, and it is deliberately uncomfortable.\n\nA community had just been given a victory beyond anything it could have arranged. Within hours it was arguing over the equipment.",
  "2": "'The ˹true˺ believers are only those whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah, whose faith increases when His revelations are recited to them, and who put their trust in their Lord.' (Quran 8:2). A definition, offered immediately after a quarrel.\n\n'Only those' — innama al-mu'minun. The word 'innama' restricts: these and no others.\n\nThat is a striking way to answer a dispute about property. Rather than ruling on the claims, the surah describes what a believer is.\n\n'Whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah' — wajilat qulubuhum. 'Wajal' is the shiver of fear mixed with awe.\n\nNotice where the description begins: inside, at the mention of a name. Not at a battlefield, not at a distribution.\n\n'Whose faith increases when His revelations are recited to them' — zadathum imana.\n\nThis clause is one of the clearest statements in the Quran that faith is not a fixed quantity. It rises. And what raises it is stated precisely: hearing the verses.\n\nAl-A'raf gave the matching instruction at 7:204: 'When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent.'\n\n'And who put their trust in their Lord' — wa 'ala rabbihim yatawakkalun. 'Tawakkul' — leaning on Him for the outcome.\n\nShu'aib said it under threat of expulsion at 7:89: 'In Allah we trust.'\n\nThree marks, and none of them is visible from outside. A man arguing over spoils and a man who has forgotten the argument entirely can look identical on a battlefield.",
  "3": "'˹They are˺ those who establish prayer and donate from what We have provided for them.' (Quran 8:3). The definition continues, and now it becomes visible.\n\nVerse 8:2 named three inward states: a trembling heart, growing faith, and trust. This verse names two outward acts.\n\n'Those who establish prayer' — alladhina yuqimuna al-salah. Not 'who pray' — who set it up, keep it standing.\n\n'And donate from what We have provided for them' — wa mimma razaqnahum yunfiqun. They spend from what We gave them.\n\nLook carefully at how that is phrased. Not 'from what they earned' — from what We provided.\n\nThat single clause is the whole answer to the dispute of 8:1. People arguing over spoils are arguing over provision that was given to them, and the definition of a believer includes giving it away.\n\n'Mimma' — from what. Not all of it. A portion.\n\nThe pairing of prayer and spending appears constantly in the Quran, and al-A'raf used it at 7:156: mercy is written for 'those who shun evil, pay alms-tax, and believe in Our revelations.'\n\nNotice the structure of these two verses. Three inward marks, then two outward ones, and the outward ones are the cheapest to fake and the easiest to verify.\n\nThe Quran gives both lists so that neither can stand alone. A trembling heart that spends nothing is unproven; a generous man with no prayer is unanchored.",
  "4": "'It is they who are the true believers. They will have elevated ranks, forgiveness, and an honourable provision from their Lord.' (Quran 8:4). The verdict on the five marks.\n\n'It is they who are the true believers' — ula'ika humu al-mu'minuna haqqa. Believers in truth.\n\nThe word 'haqqa' is added for emphasis, and it implies a distinction the community had just demonstrated: there is a difference between being counted among the believers and being one in reality.\n\nNobody at Badr was outside the faith. The verse is not excommunicating anyone; it is describing a standard.\n\n'They will have elevated ranks' — darajat. Degrees, levels.\n\nThat word answers something in the argument of 8:1. Men disputing over shares were competing for a portion of a finite pile. Here a different kind of rank is offered, and it is not distributed by committee.\n\n'Forgiveness' — maghfirah. Offered to the very people being corrected.\n\n'And an honourable provision' — rizqun karim. 'Rizq' is the same word used in the previous verse: 'from what We have provided for them.'\n\nSo the surah closes the circle deliberately. Give from the provision you were given, and receive a provision described as noble.\n\nRanks, pardon and provision — three things, and not one of them was on the field at Badr.\n\nThe next verse turns to how the battle came about, and it begins with a reminder that this community had disputed before.",
  "5": "'Similarly, when your Lord brought you ˹O Prophet˺ out of your home for a just cause, a group of believers was totally against it.' (Quran 8:5). The precedent for the argument.\n\n'When your Lord brought you out of your home' — akhrajaka rabbuka min baytika. The subject of the verb is Allah. The Prophet did not simply decide to march.\n\n'For a just cause' — bil-haqq. In truth, rightly.\n\n'A group of believers was totally against it' — wa inna fariqan mina al-mu'minina la karihun. A party of the believers disliked it.\n\nNotice the honesty of this. The Quran, recording the most celebrated victory in the Prophet's life, states plainly that a section of his own followers did not want to go.\n\nAnd it still calls them believers.\n\nThat is the reason this verse is placed here. The dispute over spoils in 8:1 was not the first disagreement; the expedition itself began with one.\n\nThe context is worth knowing. The Muslims left Madinah expecting to intercept a Meccan trade caravan, not an army. When the caravan escaped and a thousand-strong force arrived instead, the situation they faced was not the one they had signed up for.\n\nSo the reluctance was rational. Three hundred and nineteen men against a thousand is not cowardice arithmetic; it is arithmetic.\n\nAl-A'raf recorded a similar honest complaint at 7:129, when the Israelites told Musa nothing had improved since he came.\n\nThe Quran does not tidy up the people it praises.",
  "6": "'They disputed with you about the truth after it had been made clear, as if they were being driven to death with their eyes wide open.' (Quran 8:6). The reluctance described from the inside.\n\n'They disputed with you about the truth after it had been made clear' — ba'da ma tabayyan. After it had become plain.\n\nThat clause is the charge. The dispute is not about whether the matter was clear; it is a dispute that continued after clarity.\n\nAl-A'raf described the same condition at 7:101: 'they would not believe in what they had already denied.' Knowledge and agreement are not the same thing.\n\n'As if they were being driven to death' — ka annama yusaquna ila al-mawt. Driven, the way animals are driven forward.\n\n'With their eyes wide open' — wa hum yanzurun. Watching it come.\n\nThat is one of the most human images in the Quran. It does not describe cowardice; it describes clear-sightedness under dread.\n\nThey could see the numbers. They knew what a thousand armed Meccans meant, and they were walking towards them anyway.\n\nThe verse preserves the feeling without excusing the argument, and there is mercy in that combination. Fear is not treated as disbelief.\n\n'Yanzurun' — looking. The same verb al-A'raf used at 7:84 and 7:103 when the reader was told to look at how things ended for others.\n\nHere it describes men looking at what appears to be their own ending.\n\nThe next verse explains what they were actually walking into.",
  "7": "'˹Remember, O believers,˺ when Allah promised ˹to give˺ you the upper hand over either target, you wished to capture the unarmed party. But it was Allah's Will to establish the truth by His Words and uproot the disbelievers;' (Quran 8:7). What they wanted, and what they got.\n\n'Either target' — ihda al-ta'ifatayn. One of the two groups: the caravan, or the army.\n\n'You wished to capture the unarmed party' — wa tawadduna anna ghayra dhati al-shawkati takunu lakum. The one without thorns — the undefended one.\n\nThe wish is entirely reasonable. Given a choice between goods without a fight and a battle against three times your number, everyone in that camp preferred the goods.\n\nThe Quran records the preference without contempt, and then states what was actually being arranged.\n\n'But it was Allah's Will to establish the truth by His Words' — wa yuridu Allahu an yuhiqqa al-haqqa bi kalimatih. To make the truth real.\n\n'And uproot the disbelievers' — wa yaqta'a dabira al-kafirin. To cut off their last trace.\n\nThat exact phrase appears in al-A'raf at 7:72, for the destruction of 'Ad.\n\nNotice the gap between the two wishes. They wanted a caravan; they were given a battle that changed the standing of their religion in Arabia.\n\nThis is the verse to remember when a plan collapses into something harder. What was wanted was easier. What arrived was the thing that mattered.\n\nNobody in that camp could see the difference in advance, and the Quran says so plainly.",
  "8": "'to firmly establish the truth and wipe out falsehood—even to the dismay of the wicked.' (Quran 8:8). The purpose, stated in the plainest possible terms.\n\n'To firmly establish the truth' — li yuhiqqa al-haqq. To make the truth prevail, to give it standing.\n\n'And wipe out falsehood' — wa yubtila al-batil. To void the void.\n\n'Batil' — the word al-A'raf used of the magicians' ropes at 7:118, 'their illusions failed', and of the idolaters' rituals at 7:139.\n\nSurah Yunus states the same principle at 10:82: 'And Allah establishes the truth by His Words — even to the dismay of the wicked.'\n\n'Even to the dismay of the wicked' — wa law kariha al-mujrimun. Even if they hate it.\n\nNotice that this verse says nothing about the Muslims' strength, tactics or courage. The stated purpose of the day is about truth and falsehood, and the human parties are instruments in it.\n\nThat reframes the reluctance of 8:5 and 8:6 completely. The believers thought they were choosing between a caravan and an army; the choice on offer was between an easy gain and a public settling of what is real.\n\nRecall the arena in al-A'raf at 7:117 and 7:118. Pharaoh assembled a nation to watch his experts defeat Musa, and the truth was established at the event he organised.\n\nBadr is that scene again, with a different cast. Quraysh came out to protect a caravan and ended by proving something they had denied.\n\n'Karih' — hate. It is the same word used of the believers' reluctance at 8:5.",
  "9": "'˹Remember˺ when you cried out to your Lord for help, He answered, \"I will reinforce you with a thousand angels—followed by many others.\"' (Quran 8:9). The prayer, and the answer.\n\n'When you cried out to your Lord for help' — idh tastaghithuna rabbakum. 'Istighathah' is the cry of someone in real danger, not a formal request.\n\n'Umar ibn al-Khattab described the scene. The Prophet, peace be upon him, looked at the enemy — a thousand men against his three hundred and nineteen — then faced the qiblah, stretched out his hands and began calling on his Lord: 'O Allah, accomplish for me what Thou hast promised to me... O Allah, if this small band of Muslims is destroyed, Thou wilt not be worshipped on this earth.'\n\n'Umar continued: he kept calling with his hands outstretched until his cloak slipped from his shoulders, and Abu Bakr came, picked it up, put it back and embraced him from behind, saying: 'Prophet of Allah, this prayer of yours to your Lord will suffice you.' Then this verse was revealed (Sahih Muslim 1763).\n\n'I will reinforce you with a thousand angels' — bi alfin mina al-mala'ikati murdifin. Following one after another.\n\nNote what the Prophet's prayer actually argued. Not 'give us victory because we deserve it', but 'if this group is destroyed, You will not be worshipped on this earth.'\n\nHe appealed to the purpose, not to the merit.\n\nAl 'Imran mentions three thousand at 3:124 and five thousand at 3:125. The numbers differ because the reinforcement was described as continuing.",
  "10": "'And Allah made this a sign of victory and reassurance to your hearts. Victory comes only from Allah. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 8:10). What the angels were actually for.\n\n'Allah made this a sign of victory' — wa ma ja'alahu Allahu illa bushra. He made it only as good news.\n\nRead that 'only'. The thousand angels of 8:9 are described here as a message rather than a mechanism.\n\n'And reassurance to your hearts' — wa li tatma'inna bihi qulubukum. So that your hearts would settle.\n\nThat is the stated purpose: to calm three hundred frightened men.\n\nAl 'Imran says the same at 3:126 in almost identical words.\n\n'Victory comes only from Allah' — wa ma al-nasru illa min 'indi Allah. The correction is placed immediately, before anyone can start counting angels the way they had been counting men.\n\nThis is the answer to the arithmetic of 8:5 and 8:6. The problem was never the ratio. Adding an invisible army to your side does not change the principle; it only makes the principle visible.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the same realisation at 2:249: 'How many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah!'\n\n'Almighty, All-Wise' — 'azizun hakim. Powerful, and wise in how the power is used.\n\nThe wisdom is in what follows. The angels came, and the men still had to stand and fight.\n\nSupport does not remove the task. It makes the task survivable.",
  "11": "'˹Remember˺ when He caused drowsiness to overcome you, giving you serenity. And He sent down rain from the sky to purify you, free you from Satan's whispers, strengthen your hearts, and make ˹your˺ steps firm.' (Quran 8:11). Two ordinary things, on the night before the battle.\n\n'When He caused drowsiness to overcome you' — idh yughashshikumu al-nu'as. Sleepiness covered them.\n\nAnyone who has been afraid knows that sleep is the first thing fear takes. Men expecting to die in the morning do not doze.\n\n'Giving you serenity' — amanatan minh. Safety from Him.\n\nSo the first gift named on the night before Badr is a nap.\n\n'And He sent down rain from the sky' — the second gift, and the verse lists four things it did.\n\n'To purify you' — li yutahhirakum bih. Water for washing, in a place with little of it.\n\n'Free you from Satan's whispers' — wa yudhhiba 'ankum rijza al-shaytan. 'Rijz' — the word used of the plagues in al-A'raf at 7:134.\n\n'Strengthen your hearts, and make your steps firm' — wa li yarbita 'ala qulubikum wa yuthabbita bihi al-aqdam.\n\nThat last one is physical. The Muslim side of the valley was soft sand; the rain packed it down and made it walkable.\n\nNotice what this verse celebrates. Not the thousand angels — sleep, water, clean clothes and firm ground.\n\nThe help of Allah in this surah arrives mostly as weather.",
  "12": "'˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when your Lord revealed to the angels, \"I am with you. So make the believers stand firm. I will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers. So strike their necks and strike their fingertips.\"' (Quran 8:12). The angels' orders.\n\n'I am with you' — anni ma'akum. Said to the angels first.\n\n'So make the believers stand firm' — fa thabbitu alladhina amanu. Steady them.\n\nThat is the first assignment, and it matches 8:10: the reinforcement was 'reassurance to your hearts'. The angels' primary task is described as steadying human beings.\n\n'I will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers' — sa ulqi fi qulubi alladhina kafaru al-ru'b. Terror, placed inside them.\n\nNotice how the two sides are treated. One side receives firmness; the other receives fear. Nothing is said about weapons or numbers.\n\nBattles have always been decided this way. An army that breaks is beaten regardless of its strength, and an army that holds can survive odds it should not.\n\n'So strike their necks and strike their fingertips' — the command is given to the angels, and it is specific.\n\nIbn 'Abbas described what a Muslim saw that day: chasing a fleeing Meccan, he heard a whip crack above him and a rider's voice calling 'Go ahead, Haizum!' — and the man in front of him fell dead on his back, his nose scarred and his face split as if lashed. When this was reported to the Prophet, peace be upon him, he said: 'You have told the truth. This was the help from the third heaven' (Sahih Muslim 1763).\n\nSeventy of the enemy were killed that day, and seventy captured.",
  "13": "'This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger. And whoever defies Allah and His Messenger, then ˹know that˺ Allah is surely severe in punishment.' (Quran 8:13). The reason, given after the event.\n\n'This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger' — dhalika bi annahum shaqqu Allaha wa rasulah.\n\n'Shaqqa' is a strong verb. It comes from splitting: to break off, to stand on the opposite side of a division.\n\nSo the charge is not disagreement. It is separation — deliberately taking the other bank.\n\nNotice the placement. The angels' orders in 8:12 were severe, and this verse immediately supplies the ground for them.\n\nThe Quran does this repeatedly: a hard passage is followed by the reason, so that nothing in it looks arbitrary.\n\n'And whoever defies Allah and His Messenger' — the sentence then widens from the men at Badr to anyone at all.\n\nThat widening is the point of the verse. The Meccans are not a special category of people; they are an example of a rule, and the rule is stated in the open form 'whoever'.\n\nAl-A'raf did the same at 7:40 and 7:41, moving from the specific nations destroyed to the general condition that destroyed them.\n\n'Allah is surely severe in punishment' — shadidu al-'iqab.\n\nHere is what makes that clause bearable rather than crushing. Every time the Quran states it, it has just finished explaining exactly what earns it.\n\nNobody is being punished for existing on the wrong side of a battle line. They are being answered for choosing to split from what they knew.\n\nThe next verse turns from explanation to address.",
  "14": "'That ˹worldly punishment˺ is yours, so taste it! Then the disbelievers will suffer the torment of the Fire.' (Quran 8:14). Two punishments, named separately.\n\n'That is yours, so taste it' — dhalikum fa dhuquh. Here it is; try it.\n\nThe first 'that' refers to Badr itself — the defeat, the seventy dead, the seventy captured.\n\n'Then the disbelievers will suffer the torment of the Fire' — wa anna lil-kafirina 'adhaba al-nar.\n\nRead the structure carefully. The battle is one thing; the Fire is another, and the word 'then' separates them in time.\n\nThis matters because people constantly confuse the two. A worldly disaster is treated as a final verdict, and a worldly success is treated as an acquittal.\n\nAl-A'raf spent whole passages dismantling that confusion, most sharply at 7:182 and 7:183, where prosperity itself was the trap.\n\n'Dhuqu' — taste. The Quran uses this verb of punishment often, and it is not casual.\n\nTasting is the smallest possible sample of something. To be told 'taste it' is to be told that what has arrived is not the whole of it.\n\nThere is something else worth noticing. The verse is addressed to people who are, at that moment, alive.\n\nAn address implies a listener, and a listener still has time. Even 'taste it' is spoken to someone who can still hear.",
  "15": "'O believers! When you face the disbelievers in battle, never turn your backs to them.' (Quran 8:15). The first direct command of the surah to the community.\n\n'When you face the disbelievers in battle' — idha laqitumu alladhina kafaru zahfa. 'Zahf' is a slow advance, an army crawling forward in mass.\n\nThe word carries the sight of it: a line of men moving towards you at walking pace, which is far worse for the nerves than a charge.\n\n'Never turn your backs to them' — fa la tuwalluhumu al-adbar. Do not give them your backs.\n\nThis is stated as an absolute in this verse, and the very next verse supplies the exceptions. The Quran often does this: the rule first, at full strength, and then the qualification.\n\nNotice who is addressed — 'O believers'. The same people described in 8:2 with trembling hearts, and in 8:5 as reluctant.\n\nThe command is not given to men without fear. It is given because of the fear.\n\nFleeing from the battle line is treated in the Sunnah as one of the gravest sins. Abu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'Avoid the seven great destructive sins.' When asked what they were, he listed: 'To join others in worship along with Allah, to practice sorcery, to kill the life which Allah has forbidden except for a just cause, to eat up Riba, to eat up an orphan's wealth, to give back to the enemy and fleeing from the battlefield at the time of fighting, and to accuse chaste women' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2766).\n\nThe reason is structural. One man running turns a line into a rout, and the whole army dies for his single step.",
  "16": "'And whoever does so on such an occasion—unless it is a manoeuvre or to join their own troops—will earn the displeasure of Allah, and their home will be Hell. What an evil destination!' (Quran 8:16). The exceptions, and the penalty.\n\n'Unless it is a manoeuvre' — illa mutaharrifan li qital. Turning aside in order to fight.\n\nThat covers the feigned retreat, the flank, the withdrawal that repositions. Movement away from the enemy is not the offence; abandoning the fight is.\n\n'Or to join their own troops' — aw mutahayyizan ila fi'ah. Moving to join a group.\n\nSo a man falling back to a unit that is still fighting has not fled.\n\nNotice how carefully the exception is drawn. The rule of 8:15 was absolute in its wording, and within one verse the Quran distinguishes between running from a fight and moving within one.\n\nLaw given without this precision becomes cruelty. A commander who cannot order a withdrawal cannot fight at all.\n\n'Will earn the displeasure of Allah' — fa qad ba'a bi ghadabin mina Allah. He has returned carrying anger.\n\n'And their home will be Hell. What an evil destination' — wa bi'sa al-masir. The identical phrase used in an-Nisa at 4:97, for those who claimed they were too weak to move.\n\nThe pairing is worth sitting with. One verse condemns fleeing the field; the other condemns refusing to leave a place where faith cannot be practised.\n\nThe Quran is not against movement. It is against the movement that betrays the people beside you.",
  "17": "'It was not you ˹believers˺ who killed them, but it was Allah Who did so. Nor was it you ˹O Prophet˺ who threw ˹a handful of sand at the disbelievers˺, but it was Allah Who did so, rendering the believers a great favour. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 8:17). The credit is withdrawn.\n\n'It was not you who killed them' — fa lam taqtuluhum wa lakinna Allaha qatalahum.\n\nConsider when this is said. It is said to men who had just fought hand to hand and won against three times their number.\n\nEverything in that experience argues the opposite. They swung the swords; they felt the impact; they buried their own dead.\n\n'Nor was it you who threw, but it was Allah Who did so' — wa ma ramayta idh ramayta wa lakinna Allaha rama.\n\nThe reference is to the handful of dust the Prophet, peace be upon him, cast towards the Meccan lines as the battle opened.\n\nRead the Arabic order: 'you did not throw when you threw'. The act is affirmed and denied in the same breath.\n\nThat is the exact theology of this verse. The throwing happened, and the human being did it, and the result belonged to Allah.\n\nNobody is being told they did nothing. They are being told what their doing produced by itself.\n\n'Rendering the believers a great favour' — wa li yubliya al-mu'minina minhu bala'an hasana. A beautiful test.\n\nThis verse is the cure for what the whole surah opened with. Men argue over spoils because they are calculating what their effort earned.\n\nRemove the calculation and the argument dissolves.",
  "18": "'As such, Allah frustrates the evil plans of the disbelievers.' (Quran 8:18). Short, and placed exactly where it belongs.\n\n'As such' — dhalikum. That is how it is.\n\n'Allah frustrates' — wa anna Allaha muhinu kaydi al-kafirin. 'Muhin' means one who weakens, who drains the strength out of something.\n\n'Kayd' — scheming, the quiet arrangement of a plan.\n\nThe word appears throughout al-A'raf, most memorably at 7:183 where Allah says of the deniers: 'My planning is flawless.'\n\nNotice the verb chosen here. Not destroys, not blocks — weakens.\n\nThat is a precise description of what happened at Badr. The Meccan plan was not prevented; it was allowed to proceed until it collapsed under its own weight.\n\nThey marched out. They deployed. They outnumbered their enemy three to one. And it drained away.\n\n'Kayd al-kafirin' is a phrase that repays attention, because scheming is exactly what a person does when they have decided the outcome depends entirely on them.\n\nAl-A'raf recorded Pharaoh saying it at 7:123: 'This must be a conspiracy you devised in the city.' The schemer sees schemes everywhere.\n\nCompare that to the Prophet's night before the battle in 8:9 — hands outstretched, cloak slipping, asking.\n\nTwo ways of preparing for the same morning, and this verse names which of them holds.",
  "19": "'If you ˹Meccans˺ sought judgment, now it has come to you. And if you cease, it will be for your own good. But if you persist, We will persist. And your forces—no matter how numerous they might be—will not benefit you whatsoever. For Allah is certainly with the believers.' (Quran 8:19). The surah turns and speaks to the defeated.\n\n'If you sought judgment, now it has come to you' — in tastaftihu fa qad ja'akumu al-fath. 'Istiftah' is asking for a decision to be opened.\n\nBefore the battle the Meccan leadership had prayed at the Ka'bah for the more guided side to be given victory.\n\nThe verse takes them at their word. You asked for a ruling; here is the ruling.\n\nThere is something almost gentle in that. The reply does not mock the prayer — it answers it.\n\n'And if you cease, it will be for your own good' — wa in tantahu fa huwa khayrun lakum.\n\nNotice this. The audience is an enemy that has just lost seventy men, and the first thing offered is an exit.\n\n'But if you persist, We will persist' — wa in ta'udu na'ud.\n\n'And your forces, no matter how numerous, will not benefit you whatsoever' — wa lan tughniya 'ankum fi'atukum shay'an.\n\nThat clause answers the arithmetic that had frightened the believers in 8:6. Numbers were the whole basis of Meccan confidence, and numbers are exactly what is dismissed.\n\n'For Allah is certainly with the believers' — wa anna Allaha ma'a al-mu'minin.\n\nAl-A'raf said the same to Pharaoh's court at 7:127 and 7:137: the count on the field is not the count that decides.",
  "20": "'O believers! Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not turn away from him while you hear ˹his call˺.' (Quran 8:20). Back to the community, and back to the surah's first instruction.\n\nVerse 8:1 ended with 'obey Allah and His Messenger if you are true believers'. This verse repeats it, nineteen verses later, after everything Badr demonstrated.\n\nRepetition in the Quran is rarely padding. It marks the frame: the argument opened here and returns here.\n\n'And do not turn away from him' — wa la tawallaw 'anh. Do not turn your backs.\n\nLook at the verb. It is the same one used in 8:15 for fleeing the battlefield: 'never turn your backs to them.'\n\nSo the Quran describes walking away from the Messenger's instruction with the vocabulary of desertion under fire.\n\nThat comparison is the whole force of the verse. A man who hears a command and quietly does not follow it has done, in private, what the fleeing soldier does in public.\n\n'While you hear' — wa antum tasma'un. This clause is what makes it serious.\n\nNot people who missed the message. People who heard it clearly and turned anyway.\n\nAl-A'raf described the same posture at 7:79 and 7:93, where Salih and Shu'aib both said afterwards: 'I gave you advice, but you do not like those who give advice.'\n\nHearing is not the problem. Hearing without responding is.\n\nThe next verse names the people who have made an art of it.",
  "21": "'Do not be like those who say, \"We hear,\" but in fact they are not listening.' (Quran 8:21). The warning becomes a portrait.\n\n'Those who say, \"We hear\"' — qalu sami'na. The words are correct. That is what makes the verse uncomfortable.\n\n'But in fact they are not listening' — wa hum la yasma'un. And they do not hear.\n\nThe same verb is used twice with opposite meanings, and the double use is the argument. There is a hearing that registers sound and a hearing that receives instruction, and the Arabic lets one word carry both.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the contrast at 2:285, where the believers say 'sami'na wa ata'na' — we hear and we obey. Hearing there is joined to an action.\n\nHere the second half is missing, and its absence is the whole indictment.\n\n'Do not be like those' — la takunu. The command is given to believers, which means the failure is available to believers.\n\nThat is worth pausing on. The Quran does not present this as a disease of outsiders.\n\nAnyone can develop the habit: nodding through a reminder, agreeing with an obligation, feeling the warmth of agreement, and doing nothing at all.\n\nSurah Muhammad describes the polished version at 47:16 — people who listen attentively and then ask each other on the way out, 'What did he just say?'\n\nAl-A'raf placed the instruction plainly at 7:204: 'listen to it attentively and be silent, so you may be shown mercy.'\n\nSilence was required there because listening is an act, not a pause between speeches.",
  "22": "'Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are the ˹wilfully˺ deaf and dumb, who do not understand.' (Quran 8:22). A verdict, and it is severe.\n\n'The worst of all beings' — inna sharra al-dawabbi 'inda Allah. 'Dawabb' means creatures that move on the earth — every walking thing.\n\nThe choice of word is deliberate. The comparison is not with other people; it is with animals.\n\n'The deaf and dumb' — al-summu al-bukmu. Unable to hear, unable to speak.\n\nNobody thinks this describes a person with a physical disability, and the surah has already shown why. Verse 8:21 described people who say 'we hear' with working ears.\n\nThe deafness is chosen. That is the entire point.\n\n'Who do not understand' — alladhina la ya'qilun. Who do not use their reason.\n\nAl-A'raf reached the same verdict at 7:179, and in almost the same terms: people with 'hearts they do not understand with, eyes they do not see with, and ears they do not hear with... They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided.'\n\nTwo surahs, the same comparison, and both times the reason given is unused equipment.\n\nAl-Baqarah puts it as an image at 2:171: a flock that hears the shepherd's voice as noise and takes nothing from it but the sound.\n\nWhy 'worst of all creatures'? Because an animal that does not reason is doing exactly what it was built to do.\n\nA human being who does not is the only creature on earth refusing its own design.",
  "23": "'Had Allah known any goodness in them, He would have certainly made them hear. ˹But˺ even if He had made them hear, they would have surely turned away heedlessly.' (Quran 8:23). The hardest verse in the passage, and it needs care.\n\n'Had Allah known any goodness in them' — wa law 'alima Allahu fihim khayran. Any good at all.\n\n'He would have certainly made them hear' — la asma'ahum.\n\nRead that as it stands and it sounds like a locked door. But the second half of the verse explains it, and the explanation changes everything.\n\n'Even if He had made them hear, they would have surely turned away' — wa law asma'ahum la tawallaw wa hum mu'ridun.\n\nSo the withholding is not the cause of their refusal. It is a response to what they would do anyway.\n\nThat is the logic here. Allah knows not only what a person did, but what they would do under other conditions.\n\nAl-An'am states the same at 6:28: 'if they were to be sent back, they would certainly revert to what they were forbidden.'\n\n'Turned away heedlessly' — mu'ridun. The word for someone who presents you with their side and keeps walking.\n\nNotice the verb again: 'tawallaw', turned their backs, for the third time in this passage after 8:15 and 8:20.\n\nThe surah has now applied one image to three situations — the battlefield, the believer who hears and ignores, and the denier who could not be reached by any amount of hearing.\n\nThat is not accidental. It is one movement, described at three depths.\n\nThe next verse offers the opposite of turning away.",
  "24": "'O believers! Respond to Allah and His Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life. And know that Allah stands between a person and their heart, and that to Him you will all be gathered.' (Quran 8:24). After three verses on turning away, the call to turn towards.\n\n'Respond' — istajibu. Answer.\n\nAbu Sa'id ibn al-Mu'alla said: 'While I was praying in the Mosque, Allah's Messenger called me but I did not respond to him. Later I said, \"O Allah's Messenger! I was praying.\" He said, \"Didn't Allah say — Give your response to Allah and to His Apostle when he calls you?\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 4474).\n\nLook at what that exchange establishes. The man had the best excuse available — he was in prayer — and the verse still applied.\n\n'To that which gives you life' — li ma yuhyikum. What revives you.\n\nThis is the phrase that carries the verse. The call is not described as a duty, a burden or a test; it is described as the thing that makes a person alive.\n\nAl-An'am puts the same idea the other way round at 6:36: 'Only the attentive will respond. As for the dead, Allah will raise them up.'\n\n'Allah stands between a person and their heart' — anna Allaha yahulu bayna al-mar'i wa qalbih.\n\nThat clause is a warning inside an invitation. A person does not own their own heart outright, and the ability to respond today is not guaranteed tomorrow.\n\nAl-A'raf described the mechanism at 7:100 and 7:101: hearts sealed, so they will not hear.\n\nQaf states the nearness at 50:16: 'We are closer to them than their jugular vein.'\n\nAnswer while answering is still possible.",
  "25": "'Beware of a trial that will not only affect the wrongdoers among you. And know that Allah is severe in punishment.' (Quran 8:25). A warning about consequences that spread.\n\n'Beware of a trial' — wattaqu fitnah. Guard yourselves against a testing.\n\n'That will not only affect the wrongdoers among you' — la tusibanna alladhina zalamu minkum khassah. It will not strike only those who did wrong.\n\nThis overturns a comfortable assumption. People imagine that if trouble comes because of someone else's misconduct, they are safe by not participating.\n\nThe verse says otherwise, and the Sunnah explains why.\n\nAn-Nu'man ibn Bashir reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'The example of the person abiding by Allah's order and restrictions in comparison to those who violate them is like the example of those persons who drew lots for their seats in a boat. Some of them got seats in the upper part, and the others in the lower. When the latter needed water, they had to go up to bring water, so they said, \"Let us make a hole in our share of the ship, saving those who are above us from troubling them.\" So, if the people in the upper part left the others do what they had suggested, all the people of the ship would be destroyed, but if they prevented them, both parties would be safe' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2493).\n\nThat is the whole verse in a picture. The man drilling the hole is in his own section, and the water does not respect the section.\n\nNotice the wording: 'among you'. The wrongdoers are inside the community, not outside it.\n\nSo the duty implied is correction, and the surah already named it in 8:1 — 'settle your affairs'.",
  "26": "'Remember when you had been vastly outnumbered and oppressed in the land, constantly in fear of attacks by your enemy, then He sheltered you, strengthened you with His help, and provided you with good things so perhaps you would be thankful.' (Quran 8:26). A memory, addressed to people who had recently won.\n\n'When you had been vastly outnumbered' — idh antum qalilun. Few.\n\n'And oppressed in the land' — mustad'afuna fi al-ard. Made weak, treated as of no account.\n\nThe verse is describing Mecca before the migration: a small group, harassed, with no protection and nowhere to appeal.\n\n'Constantly in fear of attacks by your enemy' — takhafuna an yatakhattafakumu al-nas. Afraid people would snatch you away.\n\n'Takhattuf' is the word for a bird of prey lifting something off the ground. That is the sensation being named: living as something that can be picked up and carried off.\n\n'Then He sheltered you' — fa awakum. Gave you refuge.\n\nThat is Madinah, in a single verb.\n\n'Strengthened you with His help, and provided you with good things' — wa ayyadakum bi nasrih wa razaqakum mina al-tayyibat.\n\nNote the last item. 'Good things' — the provision, the spoils, the very property that started the argument in 8:1.\n\nThe whole surah has now circled back to its first line, and the reminder is that what they were quarrelling over is what they were given after having nothing.\n\n'So perhaps you would be thankful' — la'allakum tashkurun.\n\nAl-A'raf ends the identical sentence differently at 7:10: 'Yet you seldom give any thanks.'\n\nShu'aib made the same appeal at 7:86: 'Remember when you were few, then He increased you in number.'",
  "27": "'O believers! Do not betray Allah and the Messenger, nor betray your trusts knowingly.' (Quran 8:27). A warning about the small betrayals, placed after the reminder of rescue in 8:26.\n\n'Do not betray' — la takhunu. 'Khiyanah' is breach of a trust by someone on the inside. An enemy attacks; only a trusted person betrays.\n\nThree objects are named. Allah, the Messenger, and 'your trusts' — amanatikum.\n\nThe order runs from the largest to the most ordinary, and the verse treats them as one category. Whatever was placed in your keeping — a secret, a deposit, an office, a duty — is held on the same terms as the covenant with Allah.\n\n'Knowingly' — wa antum ta'lamun. This is the clause that gives the verse its weight. The betrayal being described is not a mistake. It is done by someone who understands exactly what he is doing while he does it.\n\nThat describes most breaches of trust accurately. Very few people are confused about whether they are keeping their word.\n\nPlacing this immediately after 8:26 is deliberate. That verse reminded them they were once few, hunted and afraid, then sheltered and provided for. A community that has just been given everything is exactly the community that starts treating its obligations loosely.\n\nThe Quran states the positive form at 4:58: 'Allah commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners.'\n\nBetrayal is what an insider does. This surah has just spent twenty-six verses making these people insiders.",
  "28": "'And know that your wealth and your children are only a test and that with Allah is a great reward.' (Quran 8:28). The reason people betray trusts.\n\nVerse 8:27 forbade betrayal. This names the two things that usually cause it.\n\n'Your wealth and your children' — amwalukum wa awladukum. Money and family. Not vices — the two things a person is expected to protect.\n\nThat is what makes the verse difficult. The Quran does not warn here about obvious temptations. It warns about the two loyalties that feel most legitimate.\n\nAlmost every trust broken in ordinary life is broken for one of these. A man takes what is not his because his family needs it; a man keeps quiet about a wrong because speaking would cost his livelihood.\n\n'Are only a test' — fitnah. A trial, something that tests what a person is made of.\n\nThe same statement appears at 64:15, and 63:9 gives the warning: 'Do not let your wealth or your children divert you from the remembrance of Allah.'\n\nNote that they are not called evil. A test is not a punishment; it is a question. Wealth and children are given, and what is being examined is what you will do when they conflict with the trust.\n\n'And that with Allah is a great reward' — wa anna Allaha 'indahu ajrun 'azim.\n\nThe second half is the answer to the first. Whatever the trust costs you in money or family standing is being weighed against something larger, held elsewhere.",
  "29": "'O believers! If you are mindful of Allah, He will grant you a standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺, absolve you of your sins, and forgive you. And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty.' (Quran 8:29). What taqwa produces.\n\n'A standard' — furqan. From the root meaning to separate. A criterion, a means of telling one thing from another.\n\nThat is precisely what 8:27 and 8:28 required. A man weighing a trust against his family's interest needs a way to see which way the matter actually falls. This verse names the faculty and says how it is acquired.\n\n'If you are mindful of Allah' — in tattaqu Allah. Taqwa comes first, and the discernment follows from it.\n\nNote the direction of that relationship, because it is the reverse of what people assume. Most of us think we need to work out the right answer before we can act rightly. The verse says the clarity is a consequence of the fear, not a prerequisite for it.\n\nThe word 'furqan' is also used in this surah at 8:41 for the day of Badr, 'the day when the two armies met' — the day that separated truth from falsehood in public.\n\nSo the believer is offered privately what Badr did publicly: a separation.\n\n'Absolve you of your sins, and forgive you' — wa yukaffir 'ankum sayyi'atikum wa yaghfir lakum. Two verbs, both about clearing the record.\n\nDiscernment, cover for past failures, and forgiveness — all attached to one condition.",
  "30": "'And ˹remember, O Prophet,˺ when the disbelievers conspired to capture, kill, or exile you. They planned, but Allah also planned. And Allah is the best of planners.' (Quran 8:30). A memory of the night before the migration.\n\nThree options were on the table: imprison him, kill him, or drive him out. The Meccan leaders met and debated which, and settled on killing him with a man from every clan so no single family could be held responsible.\n\n'They planned, but Allah also planned' — wa yamkuruna wa yamkuru Allah. The same verb for both, deliberately.\n\n'And Allah is the best of planners' — khayru al-makirin. The Quran uses the identical phrase at 3:54 about the plot against 'Isa.\n\nWhat makes this verse land where it sits is the audience. These are people who had just won at Badr and were arguing about the spoils. The verse takes them back a short distance in time, to a night when the whole enterprise came down to one man leaving a house surrounded by armed men.\n\nEverything they now possess — the standing, the property, the argument they are having — exists because that plan failed.\n\nNote also that the plot was not prevented. It was allowed to be made, allowed to be organised, and defeated at the last moment by a plan running alongside it.\n\nThat is what 'the best of planners' means here. Not that opposing plans are blocked at the start. They are permitted to run their course and are still overtaken.",
  "31": "'Whenever Our revelations are recited to them, they challenge ˹you˺, \"We have already heard ˹the recitation˺. If we wanted, we could have easily produced something similar. This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but ancient fables!\"' (Quran 8:31). The boast that was never tested.\n\n'If we wanted, we could have easily produced something similar' — law nasha'u la qulna mithla hadha.\n\nRead the conditional. If we wanted. They never did want, and the claim was never once attempted in public by people who were the finest poets of their language and who had every motive to try.\n\nThat is the weakness in the boast, and the Quran does not even bother to point it out here. It records the sentence and lets it stand.\n\n'This Quran is nothing but ancient fables' — asatiru al-awwalin. The same dismissal recorded at 6:25 and 25:5.\n\nTwo claims in one breath, and they contradict each other. If it were old stories that anybody could produce, the first claim would be unnecessary and the second would be obvious.\n\n'We have already heard' — qad sami'na. They had listened. That is worth noticing in a surah about a battle: the enemy at Badr was not ignorant of the message. Verse 6:25 described exactly this audience — people who attend the recitation and hear nothing.\n\nCalling something old is the cheapest way to avoid saying whether it is true.\n\nAnd the next verse shows what they said when they were being more honest.",
  "32": "'And ˹remember˺ when they prayed, \"O Allah! If this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us from the sky or overcome us with a painful punishment.\"' (Quran 8:32). A prayer, and it reveals more than it intends.\n\nAnas bin Malik reported that these were the words of Abu Jahl, and that verses 8:33 and 8:34 were revealed in reply (Sahih al-Bukhari 4649).\n\nLook closely at what is being asked. Not for guidance. Not for evidence. He asks for stones.\n\n'If this is indeed the truth from You' — in kana hadha huwa al-haqqa min 'indik. The condition is telling. He addresses Allah and grants, in the phrasing, that the matter is open.\n\nSo the man who called it ancient fables in 8:31 is here praying to the God whose message he denies, on the possibility that it might be true.\n\nThat is the shape of the thing. A person who was genuinely certain would not need to pray about it at all.\n\nThe request itself is a kind of dare, and dares of this kind appear throughout the Quran. Al-An'am recorded the same at 6:57 and 6:58, where the Prophet was made to say the timing was not his to give.\n\nThere is also something here about how people handle a suspicion they do not want to examine. Rather than investigate, they escalate — demanding a dramatic settlement that would end the discomfort in either direction.\n\nThe reply, in the next verse, refuses the terms entirely.",
  "33": "'But Allah would never punish them while you ˹O Prophet˺ were in their midst. Nor would He ever punish them if they prayed for forgiveness.' (Quran 8:33). The answer to the prayer of 8:32.\n\nTwo protections are named, and both were operating over the people who had just asked to be destroyed.\n\n'While you were in their midst' — wa anta fihim. The presence of the Prophet among them shielded the city. They were being kept alive by the man they were plotting to kill.\n\nThat is stated without comment, and it does not need any. Al-Anbiya' puts the general form of it at 21:107: he was sent as a mercy.\n\n'Nor would He ever punish them if they prayed for forgiveness' — wa hum yastaghfirun. The second shield, and it is the one still available to anyone.\n\nNote the structure of the whole exchange. A man asks for stones from the sky. The reply explains why the stones are not coming, and one of the two reasons is a door left open for him personally.\n\nAl-Ma'idah did the same thing at 5:74, one verse after the gravest charge in that surah: 'Will they not turn to Allah in repentance and seek His forgiveness?'\n\nThere is a hard implication in the first clause too. The Prophet would not always be among them, and the migration removed that protection. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the first shield was lifted when he left Mecca, and what followed was Badr.\n\nTwo shelters. One was temporary; the other never expires.",
  "34": "'And why should Allah not punish them while they hinder pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque, claiming to be its rightful guardians? None has the right to guardianship except those mindful ˹of Allah˺, but most pagans do not know.' (Quran 8:34). The charge, after the two protections of 8:33.\n\n'While they hinder pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque' — wa hum yasudduna 'ani al-masjidi al-haram. This was the offence. Not their beliefs — their use of the sanctuary as a weapon.\n\nThe Quran treats this as among the gravest of the Meccans' acts, and says so at 2:217: 'hindering others from the Path of Allah, rejecting Him, and expelling the worshippers from the Sacred Mosque is a greater sin in the sight of Allah.'\n\n'Claiming to be its rightful guardians' — wa ma kanu awliya'ah. And they were not.\n\n'None has the right to guardianship except those mindful of Allah' — in awliya'uhu illa al-muttaqun. The criterion is taqwa, not lineage, custody or possession.\n\nThat is a claim about what makes a person a custodian of a sacred place, and it disqualifies everyone who holds the keys without the character. The same argument appears at 9:19, which asks whether providing water to pilgrims equals faith and struggle in Allah's cause.\n\nAl-Ma'idah described what the sanctuary was for at 5:97: 'a sanctuary of well-being for all people.' A place established so that everyone would be safe had been turned into a barrier.\n\nGuardianship is measured by whether the thing guarded still does what it was made for.",
  "35": "'Their prayer at the Sacred House was nothing but whistling and clapping. So taste the punishment for your disbelief.' (Quran 8:35). What their worship actually consisted of.\n\n'Whistling and clapping' — mukan wa tasdiyah. Two words, both rare, and the early commentators explain them as literal: whistling through the fingers and clapping the hands while circling the House.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records this description of the pagan Quraysh performing tawaf naked, whistling and clapping in place of prayer.\n\nSet that against 8:34. They claimed to be guardians of the Sacred Mosque, and this is the worship they offered in it.\n\nThe point is not that noise is forbidden. It is that what they called prayer contained no content — no address to Allah, no request, no submission. It was performance.\n\nThat is a diagnosis worth taking personally. Prayer can be reduced to gesture in any community, and the reduction is rarely noticed by the people performing it.\n\nAbu Jahl, who prayed in 8:32 for stones to fall, is from this same congregation. His prayer had content; it was simply aimed at his own destruction rather than his rescue.\n\n'So taste the punishment for your disbelief' — fa dhuqu al-'adhaba bima kuntum takfurun.\n\n'Taste' — dhuqu. The verb is used because tasting is direct knowledge, and Badr had just supplied it. The people who whistled around the House had been given something to experience.",
  "36": "'Surely the disbelievers spend their wealth to hinder others from the Path of Allah. They will continue to spend to the point of regret. Then they will be defeated and the disbelievers will be driven into Hell,' (Quran 8:36). Money spent against the truth, and where it goes.\n\n'They spend their wealth to hinder others' — yunfiquna amwalahum li yasuddu 'an sabili Allah. The same verb, 'sadd', used in 8:34.\n\nThe Meccans funded the Badr expedition by public subscription, and continued to finance opposition afterwards. The verse describes an ongoing operation, not a single campaign.\n\nThen the forecast, in three stages.\n\n'They will continue to spend' — thumma yunfiqunaha. The spending goes on.\n\n'To the point of regret' — thumma takunu 'alayhim hasrah. 'Hasrah' is grief so deep it becomes a wound. The money will become a source of anguish.\n\n'Then they will be defeated' — thumma yughlabun.\n\nEvery stage is money going in and nothing coming back. That is the specific loss being described: not merely defeat, but a defeat purchased at enormous cost.\n\nContrast this with 8:28, which called the believers' wealth a test with 'a great reward' held with Allah. The same commodity, spent in two directions, producing opposite results.\n\n'Adi bin Hatim reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying that everyone will stand before Allah with no curtain and no interpreter, and Allah will ask, 'Did not I give you wealth?' — and he will say yes (Sahih al-Bukhari 1413).\n\nEveryone spends. The question is only on what.",
  "37": "'so Allah may separate the evil from the good. He will pile up the evil ones all together and then cast them into Hell. They are the ˹true˺ losers.' (Quran 8:37). The purpose behind the events of 8:36.\n\n'So Allah may separate the evil from the good' — li yamiza Allahu al-khabitha mina al-tayyib. 'Tamyiz' is sorting — putting one thing on this side and another on that.\n\nThe same two words, 'khabith' and 'tayyib', appeared in al-Ma'idah at 5:100: 'Good and evil are not equal, though you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil.'\n\nThat is the function this verse assigns to conflict. Badr, and the campaign that followed it, are described as a sorting process — and 3:179 says the same: 'Allah would not leave the believers in the condition you were in, until He distinguished the good from the evil among you.'\n\n'He will pile up the evil ones all together' — fa yarkumahu ba'dahu 'ala ba'd. Heaped, layer on layer.\n\nThe image is of things gathered into one mass because they are of one kind. Al-An'am described the same sorting at 6:129: 'This is how We make the wrongdoers allies of one another.'\n\n'They are the true losers' — humu al-khasirun. The surah's verdict on the spending described in the previous verse.\n\nNote what this does to the reader's understanding of hardship. Difficulty is not presented here as punishment or as bad luck. It is presented as a sieve.\n\nAnd a sieve does not create what passes through it. It reveals it.",
  "38": "'Tell the disbelievers that if they desist, their past will be forgiven. But if they persist, then they have an example in those destroyed before them.' (Quran 8:38). An offer made to the defeated enemy.\n\n'If they desist, their past will be forgiven' — in yantahu yughfar lahum ma qad salaf.\n\nConsider the timing. This is addressed to the survivors of Badr — men who had tortured Muslims in Mecca, driven them out, financed an army and marched on Madinah.\n\nAnd the terms are: stop, and everything before is cancelled.\n\n'Ma qad salaf' — what has already passed. The same phrase used at 5:95 about hunting violations: 'Allah has forgiven what has been done.'\n\nNothing is demanded by way of compensation. No reparations, no probation. The offer is unconditional on the past and conditional only on the future.\n\n'But if they persist, then they have an example in those destroyed before them' — fa qad madat sunnatu al-awwalin. The 'sunnah' — the established way — of the earlier peoples.\n\nAl-A'raf spent most of its length on exactly those examples: Nuh's people, 'Ad, Thamud, Madyan, Pharaoh. The reader of this verse has just come through that catalogue.\n\nSo two doors are held open and both are described honestly.\n\nMany of the men addressed by this verse did desist, and became Companions. Khalid ibn al-Walid and 'Amr ibn al-'As commanded armies at Badr's successors and later commanded Muslim ones.\n\nThe offer was real.",
  "39": "'Fight against them until there is no more persecution—and ˹your˺ devotion will be entirely to Allah. But if they desist, then surely Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.' (Quran 8:39). The objective, and the point at which fighting stops.\n\n'Until there is no more persecution' — hatta la takuna fitnah. 'Fitnah' here means persecution — the situation in which a person cannot hold a belief without being harmed for it.\n\nThat is the stated aim, and it defines the limit. The goal is not conquest, not conversion, and not the removal of other religions. It is the removal of the coercion.\n\nThe identical formula appears at 2:193, and there the limit is spelled out: 'If they stop persecuting you, let there be no hostility except against the aggressors.'\n\n'But if they desist' — fa in intahaw. The same verb as 8:38. Two verses in a row make cessation the trigger.\n\n'Then surely Allah is All-Seeing of what they do' — fa inna Allaha bima ya'maluna basir. And this closing is significant. Once they stop, their inner state is no longer the Muslims' business. Allah sees it; that is enough.\n\nNobody is to be pursued for what he believes after he has stopped fighting.\n\nAl-Ma'idah gave the standing warning against letting a grievance run past its cause at 5:2: 'Do not let the hatred of a people who once barred you from the Sacred Mosque provoke you to transgress.'\n\nA war fought to end coercion cannot continue as coercion. The verse builds its own off-switch.",
  "40": "'And if they do not comply, then know that Allah is your Protector. What an excellent Protector, and what an excellent Helper!' (Quran 8:40). The other outcome, and what the believers are told about it.\n\n'If they do not comply' — wa in tawallaw. If they turn away.\n\nThe verse does not answer with a strategy. It answers with two names.\n\n'Allah is your Protector' — anna Allaha mawlakum. 'Mawla' — the patron, the one to whom you belong and who takes your side. The word used at 6:62 for 'their True Master'.\n\n'What an excellent Protector, and what an excellent Helper' — ni'ma al-mawla wa ni'ma al-nasir.\n\nNotice which reassurance is given. Not 'you will win', not 'they are weak'. The comfort offered is a description of who is on your side.\n\nThat is consistent with the surah's account of Badr. Verse 8:10 said the angels were sent as good news, 'and there is no victory except from Allah'. The outcome was never located in the numbers.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that at Badr the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'O Allah! I appeal to You to fulfil Your Covenant and Promise. O Allah! If Your Will is that none should worship You then give victory to the pagans' — until Abu Bakr took his hand and said, 'This is sufficient for you' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3953).\n\nThat is what reliance on a Protector looked like in practice: a man who had prepared, deployed, and then stood pleading.\n\nBoth halves are required. This verse supplies the second.",
  "41": "'Know that whatever spoils you take, one-fifth is for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and ˹needy˺ travellers, if you ˹truly˺ believe in Allah and what We revealed to Our servant on that decisive day when the two armies met ˹at Badr˺.' (Quran 8:41). The answer to the question that opened the surah.\n\nVerse 8:1 recorded a dispute about the spoils and refused to settle it by arithmetic. Forty verses later, the arithmetic arrives.\n\n'One-fifth' — al-khumus. One part in five is set aside; the remaining four go to those who fought.\n\nNote who the fifth is for. Allah and the Messenger, then relatives, orphans, the poor, and travellers. Three of the five categories are people who were not on the battlefield at all.\n\nThe same list appears at 59:7, with the reason attached: 'so that wealth may not merely circulate among your rich.'\n\n'If you truly believe' — in kuntum amantum billah. The payment is tied directly to belief, not treated as a tax.\n\nThe delegation of 'Abdul Qais was told by the Prophet, peace be upon him, that believing in Allah alone meant the testimony of faith, prayer, zakat, fasting Ramadan, 'and to pay Al-Khumus' (Sahih al-Bukhari 53).\n\n'That decisive day when the two armies met' — yawma al-furqan. The day of separation. The same word used in 8:29 for the discernment given to a person who fears Allah.\n\nBadr is named as a public instance of what taqwa produces privately: a line drawn where there had been confusion.",
  "42": "'˹Remember˺ when you were on the near side of the valley, your enemy on the far side, and the caravan was below you. Even if the two armies had made an appointment ˹to meet˺, both would have certainly missed it.' (Quran 8:42). A map of the battlefield, and what it proves.\n\nThree positions. The Muslims on the near slope, the Meccan army on the far one, the trade caravan lower down and closer to the sea.\n\nNone of the three parties chose this arrangement. The Muslims came out for the caravan, as 8:5 to 8:7 recorded. The caravan escaped. The army came north to protect it and stayed to fight.\n\n'Even if the two armies had made an appointment, both would have certainly missed it' — wa law tawa'adtum la ikhtalaftum fi al-mi'ad.\n\nThat is the argument of the verse. A meeting nobody arranged happened; a meeting deliberately arranged would have fallen apart.\n\n'So Allah may establish what He had destined' — li yaqdiya Allahu amran kana maf'ula.\n\n'That those who were to perish and those who were to survive might do so after the truth had been made clear' — 'an bayyinah. Nobody died or lived that day in ignorance. The proof had been delivered first.\n\nThat clause matters for how the surah treats the enemy. This was not a fight against people who had never heard. Verse 8:31 recorded their own words: 'We have already heard.'\n\nCircumstances that look accidental are being described here as arrangement. Nobody in the valley knew that at the time.",
  "43": "'˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when Allah showed them in your dream as few in number. Had He shown them to you as many, you ˹believers˺ would have certainly faltered and disputed in the matter. But Allah spared you ˹from that˺. Surely He knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.' (Quran 8:43). Information withheld, on purpose.\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, saw the enemy in a dream, and saw them as fewer than they were. He reported what he saw, and the army marched believing it.\n\n'Had He shown them to you as many, you would have certainly faltered and disputed' — la fashiltum wa la tanaza'tum fi al-amr.\n\nTwo consequences are named, and the second is the one this surah keeps returning to. Dispute. It was there at 8:1 over the spoils, at 8:6 over going out at all, and it appears again in the next verse but one.\n\n'But Allah spared you' — wa lakinna Allaha sallam.\n\nConsider what this verse admits. The believers were not told the full size of the force facing them, because knowing it would have broken them.\n\nThat is a strikingly honest account of human capacity. It does not claim they were fearless. It says they were given only what they could carry.\n\n'Surely He knows best what is hidden in the heart' — 'alimun bi dhati al-sudur. The closing phrase explains the whole arrangement. The measure of what they could bear was taken by the One who could read it.\n\nNot every fact is a gift. Some are a weight, given only to those able to hold them.",
  "44": "'Then when your armies met, Allah made them appear as few in your eyes, and made you appear as few in theirs, so Allah may establish what He had destined. And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺.' (Quran 8:44). The same adjustment, now on the field itself.\n\nTwo viewings, in opposite directions, both reduced.\n\nThe Muslims saw a smaller enemy, so they advanced. The Meccans saw a smaller Muslim force, so they did not withdraw or send for reinforcements. Each side was made confident enough to engage.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that once the two lines closed the perception reversed for the disbelievers, and 3:13 describes what they then saw: 'The believers saw their enemy twice their number.'\n\nSo the reduction lasted exactly as long as it needed to.\n\n'So Allah may establish what He had destined' — the same clause as 8:42, repeated word for word two verses later.\n\nThe repetition is the point. The dream in 8:43, the positions in 8:42, the eyesight in 8:44 — three separate mechanisms, one purpose.\n\nNote that none of these are miracles in the dramatic sense. No sea splits. What is adjusted is what people notice, and it is adjusted just enough to move them.\n\n'And to Allah all matters will be returned' — wa ila Allahi turja'u al-umur.\n\nAnyone reviewing the day afterwards would have found only ordinary causes: a dream, a decision, a line of sight. This verse says where the accounting is actually kept.",
  "45": "'O believers! When you face an enemy, stand firm and remember Allah often so you may triumph.' (Quran 8:45). Two instructions, and the second is unexpected.\n\n'Stand firm' — fathbutu. Hold your position. Do not break.\n\n'And remember Allah often' — wa udhkuru Allaha kathiran. In the middle of a battle.\n\nThat is the surprise. Dhikr is normally associated with quiet, and here it is commanded at the loudest and most frightening moment a person can be in.\n\nThe reason is in the first instruction. Standing firm is a matter of what occupies the mind, and a mind in combat will be occupied by something. The command supplies the content.\n\n'Abdullah bin Abi Aufa reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, addressed the army: 'O people! Do not wish to face the enemy and ask Allah to save you, but if you should face the enemy, then be patient and let it be known to you that Paradise is under the shades of swords' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2965, 2966).\n\nTwo things in that hadith match this verse exactly. Do not seek the encounter; when it comes, hold.\n\n'So you may triumph' — la'allakum tuflihun. Falah is success in the widest sense, not merely winning the engagement.\n\nThe verse names conditions a person can control. Not the enemy's numbers, not the terrain — only whether he stays in place and what he keeps saying while he does.",
  "46": "'Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not dispute with one another, or you would be discouraged and weakened. Persevere! Surely Allah is with those who persevere.' (Quran 8:46). The third instruction, and the surah's central warning.\n\n'Do not dispute with one another' — wa la tanaza'u. The same verb used at 8:43 for what would have happened had the true numbers been shown.\n\nThe consequence is given in two words. 'Fatafshalu' — you lose heart. 'Wa tadhhaba rihukum' — and your wind goes.\n\nThat second image is worth pausing on. 'Rih' is wind. A ship with no wind is intact, fully crewed, and going nowhere.\n\nInternal argument is described here as a loss of momentum rather than a loss of strength. Nothing is destroyed; everything simply stops moving.\n\n'Abdullah bin 'Amr reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, heard two men arguing over a verse and came out with anger visible in his face, saying: 'Verily, the peoples before you were ruined because of their disputation in the Book' (Sahih Muslim 2666).\n\nAl 'Imran gave the historical case at 3:152: at Uhud, 'your courage weakened and you disputed about the command and disobeyed, after Allah had brought victory within your reach.'\n\n'Persevere! Surely Allah is with those who persevere' — wa isbiru inna Allaha ma'a al-sabirin.\n\nObedience, unity, endurance. The surah has now given the whole formula, and none of the three items is about equipment.",
  "47": "'Do not be like those ˹pagans˺ who left their homes arrogantly, only to be seen by people and to hinder others from Allah's Path. And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do.' (Quran 8:47). A description of the other army, given as a warning to this one.\n\nThe Meccans marched out with singing girls and slaughtered camels at each stop. Abu Jahl refused to turn back even after the caravan was safe, insisting they reach Badr and let the Arabs hear of it.\n\nThree motives are listed, and none of them is a cause.\n\n'Arrogantly' — batran. Swollen with what one has. 'Only to be seen by people' — ri'a'a al-nas. For the audience. 'And to hinder others from Allah's Path' — the charge already made at 8:34 and 8:36.\n\nThe command is 'do not be like them', which means these motives are available to believers too. An army can march for the right cause with exactly the wrong reasons in its chest.\n\nThat is the sharp edge of the verse. It arrives immediately after 8:46 warned against dispute, and the two problems are related: people who came out to be seen will argue about credit afterwards.\n\nVerse 8:1 opened with such an argument.\n\n'And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do' — muhit. Encompassing. Nothing about the expedition, on either side, is outside His knowledge.\n\nDoing the right thing to be watched doing it is a way of ruining it while it still looks correct.",
  "48": "'And ˹remember˺ when Satan made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, and said, \"No one can overcome you today. I am surely by your side.\" But when the two forces faced off, he cowered and said, \"I have absolutely nothing to do with you.\"' (Quran 8:48). The recruiter who leaves before the fighting starts.\n\n'Made their deeds appealing to them' — zayyana lahum. The same verb that ran through al-An'am at 6:43 and 6:122 — misdeeds dressed up until they look like duty.\n\nThen the guarantee: 'No one can overcome you today' — la ghaliba lakumu al-yawma mina al-nas. Followed by 'I am surely by your side' — inni jarun lakum.\n\nBoth statements were false, and the second was withdrawn within hours.\n\n'When the two forces faced off, he cowered' — nakasa 'ala 'aqibayh. He turned on his heels.\n\n'I certainly see what you do not see. I truly fear Allah, for Allah is severe in punishment.' The last words of the speech contradict everything in the first.\n\nIbrahim's surah records the same character making the same admission on a larger stage at 14:22: 'I too made you a promise, but I failed you. I did not have any authority over you. I only called you, and you responded to me.'\n\nThe pattern is consistent. Encouragement beforehand, absence at the moment of cost, and a disclaimer afterwards.\n\nAnything that promises you cannot lose is worth examining on that basis alone.",
  "49": "'˹Remember˺ when the hypocrites and those with sickness in their hearts said, \"These ˹believers˺ are deluded by their faith.\" But whoever puts their trust in Allah, surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 8:49). The commentary offered from the sidelines.\n\n'Deluded by their faith' — gharra ha'ula'i dinuhum. Their religion has fooled them.\n\nIt was, on the face of it, a reasonable assessment. Three hundred and some men, poorly armed, walking towards roughly a thousand. Anyone counting would have reached the same conclusion.\n\n'The hypocrites and those with sickness in their hearts' — al-munafiqun wa alladhina fi qulubihim marad. Two groups, or one described twice.\n\nThe same pairing speaks again at 33:12, during the siege of Madinah: 'Allah and His Messenger have promised us nothing but delusion!'\n\nNote that the speakers are not the enemy. They are inside, or at the edge, watching. Their contribution to the day was an opinion.\n\n'But whoever puts their trust in Allah' — wa man yatawakkal 'ala Allah. The reply does not argue with the arithmetic.\n\nThat is deliberate. The numbers were exactly as reported. What the counting left out was one party.\n\n'Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise' — 'azizun hakim. Powerful, and wise about when to use it — because tawakkul is not a claim that the outcome is guaranteed, but a claim about who decides it.\n\nConfidence looks like delusion to anyone who does not know what it is resting on.",
  "50": "'If only you could see when the angels take the souls of the disbelievers, beating their faces and backs, ˹saying,˺ \"Taste the torment of burning!\"' (Quran 8:50). The scene the living cannot watch.\n\n'If only you could see' — wa law tara. A phrase used when something is happening that is real but hidden.\n\nSeventy men of Quraysh died at Badr, and the surah has been discussing them as casualties and captives. This verse describes the same moment from the other side.\n\n'Beating their faces and backs' — yadribuna wujuhahum wa adbarahum. Front and back, the two directions a person in a battle turns.\n\nAn-Nisa' records another such reception at 4:97, where the angels ask the arriving souls, 'What do you think you were doing?'\n\n'Taste the torment of burning' — dhuqu 'adhaba al-hariq. 'Taste' again, the same verb used at 8:35.\n\nThe word is used because tasting is knowledge you cannot argue with. Abu Jahl had asked in 8:32 for a painful punishment as a test of whether the message was true. He received his answer, and it came too late to be useful to him.\n\nWhat this verse adds to the surah is a change of scale. Everything before it has been about a valley, a caravan, a fifth of the spoils.\n\nDeath moves the whole matter into a room the survivors were not in, and no report from that room reaches the ones still arguing over the goods.",
  "51": "'This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done. And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation.' (Quran 8:51). The sentence spoken as the punishment begins.\n\n'For what your hands have done' — bima qaddamat aydikum. What you sent ahead. The image is of goods dispatched in advance and collected on arrival.\n\nNothing has been added. Nothing has been invented against them. The verse insists on the exact correspondence between what was sent and what is received.\n\n'And Allah is never unjust to His creation' — wa anna Allaha laysa bi zallamin lil-'abid.\n\nNote the form of the word: 'zallam' is an intensive — not merely unjust, but given to injustice. The denial is emphatic, closing off the complaint before it is made.\n\nThat complaint is a real one, and this surah has been building the answer to it across many verses. Verse 8:42 said the outcome came 'after the truth had been made clear to both'. Verse 8:33 said a door of forgiveness stood open. Verse 8:38 offered the enemy a clean cancellation of everything past.\n\nEvery one of those was declined in public.\n\nAl-An'am put the same principle at 6:160 from the pleasant side: 'Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold. But whoever comes with a bad deed will be punished for only one.'\n\nReward is multiplied; punishment is measured. Only one of the two is strictly proportional, and the verse names which.",
  "52": "'Their fate is that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them—they all disbelieved in Allah's signs, so Allah seized them for their sins. Indeed, Allah is All-Powerful, severe in punishment.' (Quran 8:52). Badr placed in a much longer series.\n\n'Their fate is that of the people of Pharaoh' — ka da'bi ali fir'awn. 'Da'b' is a habit, a customary way of carrying on. Their manner was his manner.\n\nThe comparison is not flattering to the Quraysh. Pharaoh had an empire, an army and a claim to divinity. The Meccan leadership had a shrine and a caravan trade.\n\nWhat is being compared is not scale but conduct: signs delivered, signs rejected, and then the seizure.\n\n'So Allah seized them for their sins' — fa akhadhahumu Allahu bi dhunubihim. 'Akhdh' is the grip of a hand closing.\n\n'And those before them' — wa alladhina min qablihim. Al-A'raf had just walked the reader through that list at length: Nuh's people, 'Ad, Thamud, the people of Lut, Madyan.\n\nA man standing at Badr on the Meccan side would have seen a local dispute over a caravan and a preacher from his own clan. The verse tells him what he was actually standing in.\n\n'Indeed, Allah is All-Powerful, severe in punishment' — qawiyyun shadidu al-'iqab.\n\nEvery generation assumes its own situation is unprecedented. This verse says it almost never is.",
  "53": "'This is because Allah would never discontinue His favour to a people until they discontinue their faith. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 8:53). The rule underneath the whole account.\n\n'Until they discontinue their faith' — hatta yughayyiru ma bi anfusihim, until they change what is in themselves.\n\nThe sequence is fixed and the verse is careful about its order. The inward change comes first. The change in circumstances follows.\n\nAr-Ra'd states the same law in the other direction at 13:11: 'Indeed, Allah would never change a people's state ˹of favour˺ until they change their own state ˹of faith˺.'\n\nNote the word 'ni'mah' — favour, blessing. What was withdrawn from the Quraysh was not something they had earned. Mecca's security, its water, its trade and its standing among the Arabs were all given.\n\nVerse 8:26 reminded the believers of exactly this from the other side: they were few and afraid, and were sheltered and provided for.\n\nSo the same surah shows one people receiving a favour and another losing one, and locates the difference in a single place.\n\n'Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing' — sami'un 'alim. Hearing what is said, knowing what is not.\n\nThis is the most usable verse in the passage. It says decline is not imposed from outside, and that a community's condition can be read from its interior long before it shows.",
  "54": "'That was the case with Pharaoh's people and those before them—they all rejected the signs of their Lord, so We destroyed them for their sins and drowned Pharaoh's people. They were all wrongdoers.' (Quran 8:54). The same comparison as 8:52, said again with the ending supplied.\n\nVerse 8:52 named the pattern. This verse names the outcome: 'so We destroyed them' — fa ahlaknahum.\n\nThe repetition two verses apart is not accidental. Between them sits 8:53, the rule about favour and inward change. The example is stated, then explained, then stated again with its conclusion.\n\n'And drowned Pharaoh's people' — wa aghraqna ala fir'awn.\n\nDrowning is singled out because of what it undoes. Egypt's power was built on a river, and the river is what closed over them. The instrument of their prosperity became the instrument of their end.\n\n'They were all wrongdoers' — wa kullun kanu zalimin. 'Kullun' — all of them, not only Pharaoh.\n\nThat closing word removes the excuse of following. The leadership devised the policy; the people carried it out; the verdict covers both.\n\nAl-Anfal is addressed throughout to a community, not to individuals, and this is consistent with 8:25: 'Beware of a trial that will not only affect the wrongdoers among you.'\n\nA society is being described as answerable for what it permits its leaders to do in its name.\n\nThe next verses turn from Pharaoh's people to a nearer failure, and the standard does not soften.",
  "55": "'Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are those who persist in disbelief, never to have faith—' (Quran 8:55). A superlative, and it needs reading carefully.\n\n'The worst of all beings' — sharra al-dawabb. 'Dawabb' means creatures that move on the earth — every walking thing, not only people.\n\nSo the comparison is deliberately wide. Among everything that walks, this is the lowest.\n\n'Those who persist in disbelief' — alladhina kafaru fa hum la yu'minun. Two clauses. They disbelieved, and then they do not believe.\n\nThe second clause is what earns the verdict. Disbelief by itself is not what is being condemned here; the settled refusal that follows it is.\n\nNote also that this verse does not stand alone. It runs straight into 8:56, which names exactly who is meant, and the naming narrows it sharply. This is not a description of every non-Muslim.\n\nAl-An'am used a related measure at 6:38: every creature on earth and every bird in flight forms 'communities like yourselves'. Animals do what they were made to do. A person given a mind, a message, and repeated warnings, who uses all three to refuse, has fallen below the standard of a creature that has none of them.\n\nThe charge is a wasted capacity, not a wrong opinion.\n\nAnd the specific behaviour that provoked this verse is described in the next one.",
  "56": "'˹namely˺ those with whom you ˹O Prophet˺ have entered into treaties, but they violate them every time, not fearing the consequences.' (Quran 8:56). The definition of who was meant in 8:55.\n\n'Those with whom you have entered into treaties' — alladhina 'ahadta minhum. Not everyone who disbelieves. People who signed something.\n\n'They violate them every time' — thumma yanquduna 'ahdahum fi kulli marrah. Repeatedly. Not once under pressure, but as a working method.\n\nThat is the offence. The verdict in 8:55 was passed on treaty-breakers, and the surah supplies the definition in the very next breath so it cannot be misapplied.\n\n'Not fearing the consequences' — wa hum la yattaqun. No taqwa in it at all — no restraint, no sense of being answerable.\n\nAn-Nahl states the standard plainly at 16:91: 'Honour Allah's covenant when you make a pledge, and do not break your oaths after confirming them, having made Allah your guarantor.'\n\nAnd at-Tawbah is careful to protect the other case at 9:4: 'As for the polytheists who have honoured every term of their treaty with you and have not supported an enemy against you, honour your treaty with them until the end of its term.'\n\nTwo groups, two rulings, and the difference between them is conduct rather than creed.\n\nA pledge that is kept only while convenient was never a pledge. It was a delay.",
  "57": "'If you ever encounter them in battle, make a fearsome example of them, so perhaps those who would follow them may be deterred.' (Quran 8:57). What to do when a treaty-breaker takes the field.\n\n'Make a fearsome example of them' — fa sharrid bihim man khalfahum. 'Tashrid' is scattering — driving apart so decisively that everyone behind them sees it.\n\n'So perhaps those who would follow them may be deterred' — la'allahum yadhdhakkarun. That they may take heed.\n\nThe stated aim is prevention. The target of the verse is not the force in front but the ones watching from a distance who are deciding whether to do the same.\n\nThis must be read with the boundary set at 2:190: 'Fight in the cause of Allah ˹only˺ against those who wage war against you, but do not exceed the limits. Surely Allah does not like transgressors.'\n\n'If you ever encounter them in battle' — fa imma tathqafannahum fi al-harb. The condition is a battle already joined with the people described in 8:56 — those who kept tearing up agreements.\n\nPlacing this verse where it stands is significant. It comes after two verses of definition, not before them. The Quran identifies the party, states the offence, and only then gives the instruction.\n\nAnd the very next verse turns the same principle back on the Muslims themselves.",
  "58": "'And if you ˹O Prophet˺ see signs of betrayal by a people, respond by openly terminating your treaty with them. Surely Allah does not like those who betray.' (Quran 8:58). A rule of war that constrains your own side.\n\nThe situation: you have a treaty, and you have solid grounds to believe the other party is about to break it.\n\nYou may not simply act. 'Respond by openly terminating your treaty' — fa inbidh ilayhim 'ala sawa'. 'Ala sawa'' means on equal footing — both sides now know where they stand.\n\nSo the treaty must be publicly and formally ended before anything else happens. The other party is told. Nobody is struck while still believing themselves protected.\n\nConsider how much easier the alternative would be. You have evidence they are about to betray you; striking first would be cheaper and safer.\n\n'Surely Allah does not like those who betray' — inna Allaha la yuhibbu al-kha'inin. The same root, khiyanah, used at 8:27 for betraying trusts.\n\nAbu Sa'id reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'On the Day of Judgment there will be a flag fixed behind the buttocks of every person guilty of the breach of faith' (Sahih Muslim 1738a). A public marker for a private act.\n\nThe verse denies you the enemy's own method even when the enemy has used it on you.\n\nThat is the discipline. Their betrayal does not license yours; it only permits you to declare the arrangement over.",
  "59": "'Do not let those disbelievers think they are not within reach. They will have no escape.' (Quran 8:59). A reply to a calculation the enemy had made.\n\n'Think they are not within reach' — sabaqu. The verb means to get ahead, to outrun, to reach the finish first.\n\nThat is the illusion being addressed. Those who escaped Badr and returned to Mecca had, in their own reckoning, got away.\n\n'They will have no escape' — innahum la yu'jizun. 'I'jaz' is rendering someone incapable — making them unable to catch you.\n\nNobody outruns Allah. The image assumes a race, and denies that there is one.\n\nNote where this sits in the surah. Verses 8:55 to 8:58 dealt with agreements broken and how to respond. This verse addresses the confidence that breaking them carries no cost.\n\nThat confidence is what makes betrayal attractive. A person breaks a pledge because he calculates he will be gone before the consequence arrives.\n\nAl-An'am answered the same reasoning at 6:134: 'Indeed, what you have been promised will certainly come to pass. And you will have no escape.'\n\nThe word for escape there is the same.\n\nGetting away with something is a description of a short interval, not of an outcome. This verse simply extends the timeframe until the interval closes.",
  "60": "'Prepare against them what you ˹believers˺ can of ˹military˺ power and cavalry to deter Allah's enemies and your enemies as well as other enemies unknown to you but known to Allah.' (Quran 8:60). The command to be ready.\n\n'Prepare' — a'iddu. And 'what you can' — ma istata'tum. The obligation is capped at capacity, not at some absolute standard.\n\n'Uqbah bin 'Amir reported hearing the Prophet, peace be upon him, say from the pulpit: 'Prepare to meet them with as much strength as you can afford. Beware, strength consists in archery. Beware, strength consists in archery. Beware, strength consists in archery' (Sahih Muslim 1917).\n\nRepeated three times, which is how the Prophet marked something he did not want missed.\n\n'To deter' — turhibuna bihi. Deterrence, not attack. The stated purpose of the preparation is that it should not need to be used.\n\nThat reframes the entire command. A community that is visibly ready is a community less likely to be tested, and the readiness itself is the objective.\n\n'Other enemies unknown to you but known to Allah' — wa akharina min dunihim la ta'lamunahum. Threats not yet visible.\n\nThat clause makes the preparation permanent rather than reactive. You cannot prepare only for the enemy currently in front of you.\n\n'Whatever you spend in the cause of Allah will be paid to you in full and you will not be wronged' — the same wealth that 8:36 showed the enemy spending into grief.\n\nRead against 8:61, which comes next, the logic completes itself: prepare fully, and accept peace immediately when offered.",
  "61": "'If the enemy is inclined towards peace, make peace with them. And put your trust in Allah. Indeed, He ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 8:61). What to do the moment the other side leans away from war.\n\n'If they are inclined towards peace' — wa in janahu lil-salm. 'Janah' is a wing; the verb means to tilt, to lean. Not a signed offer — an inclination.\n\n'Make peace with them' — fa ijnah laha. Lean the same way. The identical verb is used for both sides, which means the Muslim response is required to mirror the movement exactly.\n\nNote that this follows immediately after the command in 8:60 to prepare everything you can. The order of the two verses is the whole teaching. Full readiness, and an immediate willingness to stop.\n\nNeither cancels the other. Preparation without willingness to make peace is aggression; willingness without preparation is not a choice, since a party that cannot fight is not offering peace but accepting terms.\n\n'And put your trust in Allah' — wa tawakkal 'ala Allah. This is where the trust is placed — in the decision to accept.\n\nThat placement answers an obvious objection. What if the offer is a trick? Peace involves lowering your guard, and the verse knows it.\n\nAn-Nisa' gives the same instruction at 4:90: 'So if they refrain from fighting you and offer you peace, then Allah does not permit you to harm them.'\n\nThe risk is acknowledged rather than denied, and the next verse deals with it directly.",
  "62": "'But if their intention is only to deceive you, then Allah is certainly sufficient for you. He is the One Who has supported you with His help and with the believers.' (Quran 8:62). The answer to the worry raised by 8:61.\n\nThe scenario is explicit. You accepted the peace, and it was a trap.\n\n'Then Allah is certainly sufficient for you' — fa inna hasbaka Allah. 'Hasb' is enough — the exact amount required, nothing lacking.\n\nNote what the verse does not say. It does not say the deception will be detected in advance, or that you were foolish to accept. It says the outcome is covered.\n\nThat is what makes the command in 8:61 workable. Accepting an offer of peace in good faith cannot be a mistake, because the risk was never yours to carry.\n\n'He is the One Who has supported you with His help and with the believers' — bi nasrihi wa bil-mu'minin.\n\nTwo supports are named, and the second is people. The help of Allah arrived at Badr partly in the form of three hundred and nineteen men who showed up.\n\nThat pairing runs through the surah. Verse 8:9 records the appeal for angels; 8:45 commands the believers to stand firm. Both are true at once.\n\nThere is a discipline here that is hard to keep. Judge the offer on its face, respond to it honestly, and leave the other party's hidden intention where it belongs.\n\nYou are answerable for your own good faith only.",
  "63": "'He brought their hearts together. Had you spent all the riches in the earth, you could not have united their hearts. But Allah has united them. Indeed, He is Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 8:63). The support named in 8:62, examined.\n\nThe believers were mentioned as a form of divine help. This verse explains why that counts as help rather than as ordinary manpower.\n\n'Had you spent all the riches in the earth, you could not have united their hearts' — law anfaqta ma fi al-ardi jami'an ma allafta bayna qulubihim.\n\nMoney buys many things, and the verse concedes the point by naming the largest possible sum. It does not buy this.\n\nThe Aws and the Khazraj had fought each other for generations, most recently at Bu'ath a few years before the migration. The Muhajirun arrived as refugees from a different city and a rival tribal system.\n\nWithin a short time they were dividing property between them.\n\nAl 'Imran describes the same thing at 3:103: 'Remember Allah's favour upon you when you were enemies, then He united your hearts, so you—by His grace—became brothers. And you were at the brink of a fiery pit and He saved you from it.'\n\nNote the practical bearing on the surah's opening. The men arguing over spoils in 8:1 were the product of this unification, and the argument put it at risk.\n\nSomething that could not be bought at any price can still be lost cheaply, over goods.",
  "64": "'O Prophet! Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you.' (Quran 8:64). One short sentence, and it is the surah in miniature.\n\n'Allah is sufficient for you' — hasbuka Allah. The same word used in 8:62 — enough, exactly enough.\n\n'And for the believers who follow you' — wa mani ittaba'aka mina al-mu'minin. The sufficiency extends to the followers, and the condition attached to them is following.\n\nThat is not decoration. The whole surah has been giving instructions: settle your affairs, do not betray trusts, stand firm, do not dispute, prepare, accept peace. Sufficiency is promised to people doing those things.\n\nConsider the position of the audience when this was revealed. They had just won at Badr, and were surrounded by a peninsula that now regarded them as a serious problem. Mecca would return; Uhud was less than a year away.\n\nInto that, one sentence: Allah is enough.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that at Badr the Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed: 'O Allah! I appeal to You to fulfil Your Covenant and Promise. O Allah! If Your Will is that none should worship You' — until Abu Bakr took his hand and said, 'This is sufficient for you' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3953).\n\nAbu Bakr used the same word, hasbuka, standing next to a man who had prepared an army and then stood pleading.\n\nSufficiency is not a substitute for effort. It is what effort rests on.",
  "65": "'O Prophet! Motivate the believers to fight. If there are twenty steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred. And if there are one hundred of you, they will overcome one thousand of the disbelievers, for they are a people who do not comprehend.' (Quran 8:65). A ratio of ten to one.\n\n'Motivate the believers' — harridi al-mu'minin. Urge them, rouse them.\n\nThen the arithmetic: twenty against two hundred, a hundred against a thousand.\n\nNote the qualifier attached to the twenty. 'Steadfast' — sabirun. The number is not twenty men; it is twenty who hold.\n\nAnd note the reason given for the enemy's weakness. Not that they are fewer, or worse armed. 'They are a people who do not comprehend' — bi annahum qawmun la yafqahun.\n\nFiqh is understanding — grasping what a thing is for. An army that does not know why it is there is being described as a fraction of its own size.\n\nThat is a claim about morale stated as a claim about numbers, and Badr had just demonstrated it. Three hundred and nineteen against a thousand, by 'Umar's count (Sahih Muslim 1763).\n\nAl-Baqarah records the same reasoning in the mouths of Talut's soldiers at 2:249: 'How many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah! And Allah is ˹always˺ with the steadfast.'\n\nWhat makes this passage remarkable is what happens in the very next verse, where the same ratio is revised downward.",
  "66": "'Now Allah has lightened your burden, for He knows that there is weakness in you. So if there are a hundred steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred.' (Quran 8:66). The ratio of 8:65 reduced from ten to one down to two to one.\n\n'Allah has lightened your burden' — khaffafa Allahu 'ankum.\n\n'For He knows that there is weakness in you' — wa 'alima anna fikum da'fa.\n\nRead those two clauses together. The standard was lowered, and the reason given is a plain statement about human limitation, delivered without any softening.\n\nVery few passages of any scripture adjust their own requirement two verses after issuing it, and state the reason as the audience's weakness.\n\nAn-Nisa' gives the general principle at 4:28: 'And it is Allah's Will to lighten your burdens, for humankind was created weak.' Al-Baqarah records the believers asking for exactly this at 2:286: 'Our Lord! Do not burden us with what we cannot bear.'\n\nThe request was made, and here is a case of it being granted in the middle of a surah about war.\n\nNote what is kept. 'Steadfast' is still in the sentence, and so is the outnumbering — two to one is still a hard standard.\n\n'And Allah is with the steadfast' — wa Allahu ma'a al-sabirin, closing exactly as 8:46 did.\n\nWhat was lowered was the ratio. What was not lowered was the requirement to stand.",
  "67": "'It is not fit for a prophet that he should take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land. You ˹believers˺ settled with the fleeting gains of this world, while Allah's aim ˹for you˺ is the Hereafter.' (Quran 8:67). A correction issued after the victory.\n\nSeventy men of Quraysh were taken prisoner at Badr, and the question was what to do with them.\n\n'Umar bin al-Khattab reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, consulted him and Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr advised taking ransom, since 'they are our kith and kin' and Allah might guide them. 'Umar advised execution. The Prophet took Abu Bakr's view. The next day 'Umar found the Prophet and Abu Bakr weeping, and was told: 'I weep for what has happened to your companions for taking ransom' — and then this verse was revealed (Sahih Muslim 1763).\n\n'You settled with the fleeting gains of this world' — turiduna 'arada al-dunya. 'Arad' is something incidental, passing across your path.\n\nThe correction is not aimed at cruelty. It is aimed at taking the profitable option at a moment that called for something else.\n\n'While Allah's aim for you is the Hereafter' — wa Allahu yuridu al-akhirah. Two wills, wanting different things.\n\nNote who is corrected. The Companions at Badr, immediately after the greatest day of their lives, over a decision made with good intentions on the advice of Abu Bakr.\n\nThat the Quran records this, rather than omitting it, is itself an argument about the book's origin.",
  "68": "'Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, you would have certainly been disciplined with a tremendous punishment for whatever ˹ransom˺ you have taken.' (Quran 8:68). How serious the matter of 8:67 was.\n\n'A prior decree from Allah' — kitabun mina Allahi sabaq. Something written beforehand.\n\nThe early commentators explain this decree in more than one way. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the view that spoils had already been decreed lawful for this community, and the view that it was Allah's prior decision not to punish for something done before the ruling came.\n\nEither way, the shelter is not something they earned on the day.\n\n'You would have certainly been disciplined with a tremendous punishment' — la massakum 'adhabun 'azim.\n\nThat is a severe sentence, and it is worth being clear about what provoked it: an error of judgment on a matter for which no instruction had yet been given.\n\nThe standard applied to the first community was extremely high, and the surah does not hide it.\n\nThen note the mercy in the mechanism. A decree made in advance stood between them and the consequence. Verse 8:33 described the same structure for the Meccans: 'Allah would never punish them while you were in their midst.'\n\nBoth communities were being sheltered by something they did not arrange.\n\nAnd the next verse, remarkably, tells them to go ahead and use what they took.",
  "69": "“Now enjoy what you have taken, for it is lawful and good. And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” lifts the weight after the prior decree spared the believers from tremendous punishment for the ransom. The opening “Now enjoy what you have taken, for it is lawful and good” or “fa-kuloo mimma ghanimtum halalan tayyiba” uses “kuloo,” eat, enjoy, the same eating permitted for garden fruits, cattle fit for labour, and twel ve springs’ tayyibat where they ate with thanks. “Halalan tayyiba,” lawful and good, is the opposite of the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven,” the impure seized by successors, the fleeting goods, and the made-up shares for associates. Spoils are now declared halal tayyib by the prior decree, making them like the manna and quails where they were told “Eat from the good things We have provided,” not like the stone-prayer that sought punishment.\n\nThe command “And be mindful of Allah” or “wa ittaqoo Allah” is the same taqwa that is the best clothing, that makes the infertile produce, that gives furqan, that makes the Home of the Hereafter far better, the taqwa that the poor believers used to invoke morning and evening and that the Heights hoped to enter. Mindfulness after enjoyment prevents the new lawful gain from becoming the next fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers, from becoming betrayal of trusts knowingly, and from becoming dispute that discourages and makes wind go.\n\nThe names “Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “inna Allaha Ghafoorun Raheem” repeat the mercy that encompassed the seventy after the earthquake their foolish did, the calf worshippers whose deeds became vain but who could repent and become believers to find Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, and the successorship test where hasanah in both worlds is written for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe. Forgiveness here covers the preference for near over Hereafter that prompted the captives, and mercy makes the spoils, like the fifth for orphans, poor, and travellers, a means of honourable provision rather than disgrace.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats lawful after pardon as unrestricted indulgence. The verse joins halal tayyib with ittaqoo, ensuring enjoyment remains within the just measure, the gracious enjoining of right, and the turning from the ignorant, so the trial of wealth and children, the fitnah of spoils, becomes the thankful remembrance of shelter when few and oppressed, and the elevation that remains is rank, forgiveness, and karim provision from the Lord who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  "70": "“O Prophet! Tell the captives in your custody, ‘If Allah finds goodness in your hearts, He will give you better than what has been taken from you, and forgive you. For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful’” turns from the believers’ enjoyment to the captives’ future. The previous verse permitted enjoyment as halal tayyib with mindfulness, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. Now the captives taken until the land was subdued are addressed. The opening “O Prophet! Tell the captives in your custody” or “ya ayyuha al-nabiyyu qul li-man fi aidikum mina al-asra” shows the Prophet as warner and bringer of good news, the same warner who motivates to fight and who is sufficient for believers. “If Allah finds goodness in your hearts, He will give you better than what has been taken from you, and forgive you” or “in ya‘lami Allahu fi quloobikum khairan yu’tikum khairan mimma ukhitha minkum wa yaghfir lakum” centres on khair, goodness, the same khair whose absence made Him not make them hear even if heard they would turn heedlessly, and whose presence makes fertile land produce abundantly, makes faith increase, makes remembrance constant. Goodness here includes inclination to truth, recognition of the unlettered Prophet whose description they find in Torah and Gospel, and desire for the guidance that relieves burdens.\n\nThe promise “He will give you better than what has been taken from you” replaces the fleeting goods of this world, the arad al-dunya and the fifth’s share, with better, like the tenfold honourable provision and the hasanah in both worlds written for the mindful, the better Home far better than lowly gain seized. The second promise “and forgive you” uses the same forgiveness the calf cohort needed after remorse lest they be losers and Moses sought for himself and brother as most merciful of the merciful, showing surrender can turn ransom loss into sin forgiveness, as with those who commit evil then repent and believe to find All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThe names “For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” seal, as after eating lawful and good, emphasizing that captivity is not final damnation but a trial where Allah stands between person and heart and to Him all will be gathered, and where wealth and children are test.\n\nA common error views captives as merely ransom revenue. The verse views them as hearts where goodness may be found, inviting them to the same “We will be forgiven” not as false claim while seizing again but as true forgiveness after goodness found, guiding the community that was told to firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted to treat captives with the gracious, just invitation rather than the whistling, hindering way.",
  "71": "“But if their intention is only to betray you ˹O Prophet˺, they sought to betray Allah before. But He gave you power over them. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” balances the previous promise of better if goodness is found with the warning if betrayal remains. The verse says “But if their intention is only to betray you O Prophet, they sought to betray Allah before. But He gave you power over them” or “wa in yureedo khiyanataka fa-qad khanoo Allaha min qablu fa-amkana minkum.” The khiyanah, betrayal, echoes “Do not betray Allah and Messenger, nor betray trusts knowingly” and “If you see signs of betrayal, openly terminate on equal footing” and “Allah does not like betrayers.” Captives’ betrayal would be like the treaty violators who violate every time without taqwa, the worst beings who persist never to have faith, the Sabbath tricksters, the gate changers. The phrase “they sought to betray Allah before” links their present intent to prior betrayal of the covenant to speak only truth and to worship alone, the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” and the mountain covenant, and to the specific betrayal of supporting the enemy against the mindful while claiming to be guardians of the Sacred House.\n\nThe counter “But He gave you power over them” or “fa-amkana minkum” uses “amkana,” to give enablement, to make possible, the same enablement that made believers few over many, made hearts united when riches could not, made rain purify and angels firm steps, made truth prevail and illusions fail, made the sea a road and then a grave. Enablement is the other face of the fifth’s distribution and the valley’s appointment: Allah’s power, not the captives’ ransom, decided Badr.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem” close, as with the dream few where He knows best what is hidden in the heart and with the lightening of burden knowing weakness, and with victory only from Allmighty, All-Wise. He knows goodness in hearts versus betrayal intent, and His wisdom decides to give better and forgiveness or to give power over betrayers.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks captivity’s outcome rests on the captor’s leniency alone. The verse says outcome rests on Allah’s enabling based on knowledge of betrayal past and present, inviting captives to choose the goodness that finds better and forgiveness rather than the betrayal whose power has already been shown to be frustrated, and inviting believers dividing spoils to trust the All-Knowing, All-Wise whose creation and command are His rather than betraying trusts for near gain.",
  "72": "“Those who believed, emigrated, and strived with their wealth and lives in the cause of Allah, as well as those who gave them shelter and help— they are truly guardians of one another. As for those who believed but did not emigrate, you have no obligations to them until they emigrate. But if they seek your help ˹against persecution˺ in faith, it is your obligation to help them, except against people bound with you in a treaty. Allah is All-Seeing of what you do” defines the new social order after the captives and spoils, organizing the community that was just promised help, heart-union, and suffisance. The first group “Those who believed, emigrated, and strived with their wealth and lives in the cause of Allah” are the Muhajirun, who left homes like the Prophet was brought out for a just cause, who strived with amwal and anfus as wealth and children test, and “as well as those who gave them shelter and help—they are truly guardians of one another” are the Ansar, who sheltered, or “awaw,” and helped, or “nasaroo,” after being few and oppressed then sheltered and strengthened with tashkuroon. Their guardianship, or “awliya,” contrasts with the treaty breakers whose guardianship is betrayal and with the devils who are allies of those who disbelieve. True guardianship is the fertile guardianship of the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and of the believer who responds to what gives life.\n\nThe second group “As for those who believed but did not emigrate, you have no obligations to them until they emigrate” addresses Bedouin believers who stayed, lacking the trial that proves faith as the believers’ dispute as if driven to death proved, like those who inherit Scripture but do not firmly abide. No wilayah obligation means no mutual defence and spoil share until hijra completes the test.\n\nThe exception “But if they seek your help against persecution in faith, it is your obligation to help them, except against people bound with you in a treaty” balances the law of treaties and fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers: help in faith is obligatory, but not by violating an equal-footing treaty openly terminated if betrayal is feared, because Allah does not like betrayers and loves the mindful who honour terms until end.\n\nThe seal “Allah is All-Seeing of what you do” repeats the “All-Seer” after the mountain, the “never absent” after the full account, the “encompassing” of what they do when leaving homes to be seen. A common error treats emigration as mere geography. The Quran shows it is commitment that creates wilayah, the earth belonging to Allah to grant to whom He chooses, so that help, spoils’ fifth, and deterrent preparation all serve a community where true guardianship is ranked by belief, emigration, striving, shelter, and help, not by lowly gain seized.",
  "73": "“As for the disbelievers, they are guardians of one another. And unless you ˹believers˺ act likewise, there will be great oppression and corruption in the land” gives the counterpart to the believers’ guardianship, defining the opposing alliance that makes Badr’s help necessary. The previous verse described Muhajirun and Ansar who guarded each other as true awliya, with exceptions for non-emigrants except when persecuted and treaty-bound. This verse says “As for the disbelievers, they are guardians of one another” or “wa allatheena kafaroo ba‘duhum awliya’u ba‘d” some of them are allies of others, like the earlier worst beings who persist, the treaty violators, the hinderers who spend to hinder and wage whistling prayer, the Satan who beautifies and deserts, the hypocrites with sickness who call believers deluded, all guardians of each other against the mindful. This is the same “wrongdoers are allies of one another because of misdeeds” and “devils are allies of those who disbelieve” and “We dispersed them in groups some righteous others less so” complexity.\n\nThe warning “And unless you believers act likewise, there will be great oppression and corruption in the land” or “illa taf‘aloohu takun fitnatun fil-ardi wa fasadun kabeer” uses “fitnah,” the same trial that will not only affect wrongdoers and that is wealth and children, now as great oppression, and “fasad kabeer,” great corruption, the same great corruption after the land was set in order that rain and angels and firm steps were sent to prevent. If believers do not guard each other as devils guard each other, the earth that belongs to Allah will be filled with the whistling, the calf lowing, the Sabbath fish tricks, the gate word changes, the market defrauding, the ambush on every road making the Way seem crooked—exactly what Badr was to uproot so devotion would be entirely for Allah.\n\nThe verse thus justifies the fifth, the deterrent preparation of power and horses to terrify enemies known and unknown, and the ratios of steadfastness, all as means to ensure wilayah of believers prevails over wilayah of disbelievers.\n\nA common misunderstanding praises neutral non-alignment between the two guardian-ships. The Quran says neutrality leaves fitnah and great fasad, as the silent middle of the Sabbath town was not praised as rescued. The fertile community must mirror the disbelievers’ mutual aid with its own, as the hearts that Allah united must remain united, firm, remembering often, obeying, not disputing, persevering with Allah who is with those who persevere, so the land’s order remains and the Hour’s tremendous surprise does not find oppression reigning because the mindful neglected to guard each other.",
  "74": "“Those who believed, migrated, and struggled in the cause of Allah, and those who gave ˹them˺ shelter and help, they are the true believers. They will have forgiveness and an honourable provision” repeats the guardianship portrait with its reward to seal the Badr surah’s ethic before the final inheritance rule. The previous verses warned unless believers act as mutual guardians there will be great oppression and corruption, and defined the two guardian-ships. This verse repeats “Those who believed, migrated, and struggled in the cause of Allah, and those who gave them shelter and help, they are the true believers” or “allatheena amanoo wa hajaroo wa jahadoo fi sabeeli Allaha wa allatheena awaw wa nasaroo ula’ika humu al-mu’minoon haqqa,” the same true belief, or “haqqa,” earlier defined by trembling hearts, increasing faith, trust, establishing prayer, and donating, the true belief that earns elevated ranks.\n\nThe reward “They will have forgiveness and an honourable provision” or “lahum maghfiratun wa rizqun kareem” repeats the honourable provision promised for true believers at the surah’s start, after the spoils’ fifth for orphans, poor, travellers, after the rain that purified and firm steps, after the thousand angels bushra. “Maghfirah” is the covering the calf worshippers sought when they realized they strayed, and that Moses sought for himself and his brother as most merciful, and that was promised to captives if goodness is found. “Rizq kareem” is the tayyibat provided when sheltered and strengthened so perhaps you would be thankful, now made honourable, generous, lasting, unlike the fleeting gains of this world the believers were told not to settle for and the lowly gain successors seize.\n\nThe repetition after the guardianship warning shows that honourable provision is not automatic with emigration alone but with the chain: belief plus emigration plus striving with wealth and lives as the test of amwal and awlad, plus shelter and help that is wilayah, the heart-union riches could not buy.\n\nA common error separates true belief from struggle and shelter. The verse joins them, as the earth that belongs to Allah and outcome for the righteous requires both striving and sheltering, both the staff thrown and the sea crossed, both the dream few and the double fewness. For the spoils dividers, the honourable provision redefines spoils: the fifth’s distribution is not the whole provision; the greater provision is the forgiveness and karim rizq that comes from being true believers who, when called to what gives life, respond while Allah stands between person and heart and to Him all will be gathered, and who, when they face an enemy, stand firm, remember often, obey, and persevere.",
  "75": "“And those who later believed, migrated, and struggled alongside you, they are also with you. But only blood relatives are now entitled to inherit from one another, as ordained by Allah. Surely Allah has ˹full˺ knowledge of everything” closes the guardianship order with a final inclusion and a boundary. The previous verse gave the core true believers—Muhajirun who believed, emigrated, struggled, and Ansar who sheltered and helped—their forgiveness and honourable provision. This verse adds the later comers: “And those who later believed, migrated, and struggled alongside you, they are also with you” or “wa allatheena amanoo min ba‘du wa hajaroo wa jahadoo ma‘akum fa-ula’ika minkum.” “Min ba‘du,” later, after Badr, after the spoils’ fitnah and the trial of wealth and children, still counts if the same belief, hijra, and jihad are made, showing succession remains open, like the hasanah written for this world and the next for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe and follow the unlettered Prophet, and like the green fruit produced from lifeless land when winds drive heavy clouds.\n\nThe boundary “But only blood relatives are now entitled to inherit from one another, as ordained by Allah” or “wa ulu al-arhami ba‘duhum awla bi-ba‘din fi Kitabi Allah” abrogates the earlier brotherhood inheritance based on faith alone where Muhajirun and Ansar inherited from each other, now restricting inheritance, or “irth,” to blood kin, or “ulu al-arham,” womb relatives, as ordained in Allah’s Book. This restores the primordial created-from-single-soul kinship within the new faith community, balancing the earlier “Those with whom you have treaties violate every time” by limiting treaty-like inheritance to its proper place. The spoils’ fifth remains for the community’s orphans, poor, travellers, but family inheritance returns to arham, preventing the confusion that faith abrogates natural ties or that near gain can be seized as inheritance without blood.\n\nThe seal “Surely Allah has full knowledge of everything” or “inna Allaha bi-kulli shay’in aleem” repeats the All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in hearts, what is between person and heart, who knows goodness that makes hearing possible, who knows best what is hidden, whose knowledge encompasses the fifth, the valley sides, the dream, the double fewness, the betrayals openly terminated, the spending that will be paid in full, and the angelic bushra. A common misunderstanding freezes the early brotherhood inheritance as permanent. The verse shows the Book explains with knowledge, varying lessons for the thankful, and can ordain a new rule that honors both the wilayah of believers that prevents great oppression and the arham that the mountain covenant to hold firmly and the remembrance when few and oppressed must also honor, keeping the earth Allah grants to whom He chooses orderly before the matters returned for judgment."
};
